<h2>The transaction and its unravelling</h2>
<p>The trust&rsquo;s process was, on the record, elaborate: a trustee resolution, public notices, three bids for each parcel, two valuations, and sanction orders under Section 36(1)(a) of the Maharashtra Public Trusts Act that ran through the interest, benefit and protection of the trust. The purchaser paid, a conveyance for the Vadavali parcel was registered in September 2024, and part of the land was surrendered to the Thane Municipal Corporation under an indenture of February 2025.</p>
<p>Then a former trustee invoked Article 227. In March 2025, the Bombay High Court quashed both sanctions, annulled the registered conveyance, and remitted the applications for fresh decision. The purchaser, the trust&rsquo;s counterparties and the Corporation all reached the Supreme Court.</p>

<h2>The supervisory line, redrawn</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>What Article 227 permits</strong><br>Correcting jurisdictional error, perversity, or a failure of the tribunal or authority to act within the bounds of its power.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>What the High Court did</strong><br>Re-weighed bids and valuations, recorded findings contrary to the record, and substituted its own assessment of the trust&rsquo;s interest for the Commissioner&rsquo;s reasoned satisfaction.</div>
</div>
<p>The Supreme Court held the second course impermissible. The Commissioner&rsquo;s order was reasoned: it accounted for the tender process and for the valuers&rsquo; negative weightages reflecting reservation, coastal-zone and forest constraints on the land. Disagreement with a plausible, reasoned outcome is the stuff of appeals — and Article 227 creates none.</p>

<h2>Finality after conveyance</h2>
<p>The second strand of the ruling may matter even more to conveyancing practice. The proviso to Section 36(2) of the Maharashtra Act bars revocation of a sanction once the conveyance is executed, save for fraud practised on the Charity Commissioner himself. No fraud was pleaded. The Court also noted that the respondent had a statutory remedy under Section 36(2) which he never used. Transactions completed on the faith of a statutory sanction are not to be unsettled by supervisory petitions filed thereafter — a principle with obvious resonance for purchasers of trust, endowment and institutional land everywhere.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Protection the ruling confirms</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Before sanction</td><td>Objectors participate before the Charity Commissioner; his satisfaction must be reasoned</td></tr>
  <tr><td>After sanction, before conveyance</td><td>Statutory revocation route under Section 36(2) remains open</td></tr>
  <tr><td>After conveyance</td><td>Revocation only for fraud on the Commissioner; Article 227 cannot be used as a substitute appeal</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">This report summarises a judgment of the Supreme Court of India dated 26 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice.</div>
